.TH JITTERENTROPY-RECORD 3 2026-09-30 "jitterentropy x.y.z" "Library Functions Manual"
.\" groff re-enables hyphenation per paragraph unless HY is 0.
.nr HY 0
.nh
.SH NAME
jitterentropy-record, jent_record_raw, jent_record_run, jent_record_report_len, jent_record_health_names, jent_record_alloc, jent_record_prime, jent_record_sample, jent_record_free \- record raw noise data of the CPU Jitter RNG
.SH LIBRARY
Jitter RNG recording library
.RI ( libjitterentropy-record ", " \-ljitterentropy-record ).
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <jitterentropy-record.h>
.sp
.BI "int jent_record_raw(const char *" pathname ", unsigned int " rounds ,
.BI "                    unsigned int " osr ", unsigned int " flags ,
.BI "                    unsigned int " source ", unsigned int " loopcnt ,
.BI "                    struct jent_record_result *" result ,
.BI "                    char *" status ", size_t " statuslen );
.sp
.BI "int jent_record_run(const struct jent_record_config *" config ,
.BI "                    char *" report ", size_t " reportlen );
.sp
.BI "size_t jent_record_report_len(const struct jent_record_config *" config );
.sp
.BI "size_t jent_record_health_names(unsigned int " health_failure ,
.BI "                                char *" buf ", size_t " len );
.sp
.BI "int jent_record_alloc(struct jent_record **" rec ", unsigned int " osr ,
.BI "                      unsigned int " flags ", unsigned int " source );
.BI "void jent_record_prime(struct jent_record *" rec ", unsigned int " loopcnt );
.BI "uint64_t jent_record_sample(struct jent_record *" rec ,
.BI "                            unsigned int " loopcnt );
.BI "void jent_record_free(struct jent_record *" rec );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The library records the raw time deltas of the noise source of the CPU Jitter
Random Number Generator, the data an SP800-90B or NTG.1 assessment takes,
from within a program - for a process no recording tool can run in, such as
an Android or iOS app.
.BR jitterentropy-hashtime (1)
records through the same code.
.PP
It carries a copy of
.I libjitterentropy
of its own and exports nothing but the functions below, so it links beside
.I libjitterentropy
without either seeing the other. The flags a recording takes are those of
.IR jitterentropy.h .
.PP
It is not for production: a recording bypasses the startup tests and runs
the noise source without conditioning its output.
.PP
.BR jent_record_raw ()
records
.I rounds
time deltas into the file
.IR pathname ,
one decimal value per line, on a collector allocated with
.I osr
and
.IR flags ,
as
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (3)
takes them, but without the startup, so that the configuration recorded is
the one asked for.
.I source
selects the noise source:
.B JENT_RECORD_COMMON
for the whole measurement,
.B JENT_RECORD_HASHLOOP
for the hash loop and
.B JENT_RECORD_MEMACCESS
for the memory access loop alone. A
.I loopcnt
other than 0 replaces the loop count of the collector.
.IR result ,
where not NULL, receives what the collector ran with and the health test
failures of the recording, which do not end it:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
struct jent_record_result {
    unsigned int health_failure; /* JENT_*_FAILURE bits */
    unsigned int memsize;        /* memory access region, bytes */
    unsigned int hashloopcnt;    /* hash loop count */
    unsigned int internal_timer; /* 1: the timer thread's stamps */
};
.EE
.in
.PP
.IR status ,
where not NULL, receives the status document of that collector;
.B JENT_RECORD_STATUS_LEN
bytes hold it.
.PP
.BR jent_record_run ()
is
.BR jitterentropy-hashtime (1)
driven by a structure instead of a command line: it pins the calling thread
as asked and records
.I repeats
files through
.BR jent_record_raw ().
What the tool prints is written to
.IR report ;
.BR jent_record_report_len ()
returns a
.I reportlen
that holds all of it.
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
struct jent_record_config {
    const char *file;      /* <file>\-0001.data and so on */
    unsigned long rounds;  /* time deltas per file */
    unsigned long repeats; /* files, each from a new collector */
    unsigned int flags;    /* JENT_* flags */
    unsigned int osr;      /* 0: the default */
    unsigned int loopcnt;  /* 0: the collector's own */
    unsigned int source;   /* JENT_RECORD_* */
    int cpu;               /* \-1: not pinned */
    unsigned int cores;    /* JENT_RECORD_CORES_* */
    int status;            /* print the status, record nothing */
};
.EE
.in
.PP
.I cores
is
.BR JENT_RECORD_CORES_ANY ,
or on Apple platforms
.B JENT_RECORD_CORES_EFFICIENCY
or
.BR JENT_RECORD_CORES_PERFORMANCE .
The memory lock limit and the secure memory of the compliance modes are the
caller's to set up before the call.
.PP
.BR jent_record_health_names ()
writes the names of the health tests in
.IR health_failure ,
such as "RCT APT-permanent", into
.IR buf .
.PP
.BR jent_record_alloc ()
allocates in
.I rec
a recording that writes no file: the caller takes one delta at a time from
.BR jent_record_sample (),
on a collector allocated as for
.BR jent_record_raw ().
.BR jent_record_prime ()
takes the time stamp the next delta is measured from, at the
.I loopcnt
of the samples to follow; it is needed before the first sample and after any
pause that is not to be recorded, and does nothing for the hash loop or
memory access loop alone. Unlike
.BR jent_record_raw (),
the deltas keep the division by the timer GCD.
.BR jent_record_free ()
releases the recording. The debugfs test interface of the kernel module
records through these.
.PP
None of the functions prints anything, and none is safe to call concurrently
with another call into the library.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR jent_record_raw ()
returns one of:
.TP
.BR JENT_RECORD_OK " (0)"
The file was recorded.
.TP
.BR JENT_RECORD_EINVAL " (1)"
No such noise source, no rounds, or the memory access loop with memory
access disabled.
.TP
.BR JENT_RECORD_ENOMEM " (2)"
No memory for the samples.
.TP
.BR JENT_RECORD_ECOLLECTOR " (3)"
No collector: the self tests of the conditioning failed, the library refuses
the flags, or memory could not be allocated or, in a compliance mode, locked.
.TP
.BR JENT_RECORD_EIO " (4)"
The file could not be written;
.I errno
says why, and nothing is left of it.
.PP
.BR jent_record_alloc ()
returns the same codes, with
.B JENT_RECORD_EINVAL
also for an
.I osr
above
.B JENT_MAX_OSR
or flags
.BR jent_flags_invalid ()
refuses, and
.BR JENT_RECORD_ESELFTEST " (5)"
where the self tests of the conditioning failed.
.BR jent_record_sample ()
returns the delta.
.PP
.BR jent_record_run ()
returns the exit status of the tool, 0 or 1.
.BR jent_record_health_names ()
returns the length the whole list needs, as
.BR snprintf (3)
does.
.SH NOTES
The build offers the library only when asked to:
.I \-DENABLE_RECORDING=ON
with CMake,
.I ENABLE_RECORDING=1
with make.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jitterentropy-hashtime (1),
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (3),
.BR jent_status (3),
.BR jitterentropy (3)
